/*
 * model_params.def - the named modelling parameters of the machine, defined once (plan 03-17; D-105, D-113, D-134).
 * A parameter names a behaviour that no manual page settles and that the project implements in more than one way: a modelling assumption, never a fact.
 * One X-macro row per parameter: DS_MODEL(name, allowed, default, ledger, status)
 *   name     the parameter name; it is also the name of the one-byte member of struct ds_instance that holds the selection (an index into the allowed list), and the key model.NAME of a run request
 *   allowed  the permitted values separated by a vertical bar; the FIRST value is the default (a zeroed instance holds index 0), so a booted machine needs no initialiser
 *   default  the default value as text; it must equal the first allowed value (the conformance case model_param_refusal checks it)
 *   ledger   the ledger id that governs the parameter (the entry is written by plan 03-18)
 *   status   assumed or open: never a supported claim (ER-2)
 * A row exists only because both values are implemented and a fixture distinguishes them (D-113); at most 4 rows in Phase 3. The selection is set only by ds_model_set, before the first step; it is machine state and travels in
 * the snapshot region that owns the behaviour (tma_zero in BUS0, D-121). A change of a default bumps DS_BEHAVIOUR_EPOCH. Everything that depends on the rows (the index enum, the name, allowed, default, ledger and status tables,
 * the setter) is generated from this file: no consumer keeps its own copy.
 *
 * tma_zero   TMA with the operand $00: bus_latch returns the one-byte latch mpr_latch (the last value that TAM or TMA moved), a_unchanged leaves A alone. The manual defines only the one-hot operand (S8-101).
 */
DS_MODEL(tma_zero, "bus_latch|a_unchanged", "bus_latch", "LED-CPU-0015", "assumed")
